"The occasion of revelation for the initial verses of Surah al-Mumtahanah was that a Companion named Hatib ibn Abi Balta'ah secretly sent a letter to the polytheists during the preparations for the conquest of Mecca. Since Hatib had no kinship ties to the Quraysh, he wrote a letter informing them of the Prophet's preparations for war in order to protect his relatives in Mecca; however, Allah informed the Prophet of the situation through Gabriel (Jibril). Thereupon, Ali, Zubayr, and Miqdad were dispatched and intercepted the letter; Hatib was interrogated and pardoned on the basis of the hadith in which the people of Badr were given glad tidings of forgiveness.\n\nUpon this incident, the first verse states: 'O you who have believed! Do not take My enemies and your enemies as allies.' What is meant here are the disbelievers who are the enemies of Allah and the enemies of the believers. The verse forbids the believers from harboring affection for them and disclosing secrets to them. For they have disbelieved in the truth (the Quran and faith) that has come to you, and have expelled the Prophet and yourselves from your homeland simply because you believe in Allah, your Lord. If you have gone out to strive in the cause of Allah and to seek His pleasure, do not take those enemies as allies.\n\nWith the expression 'you show affection to them,' the verse refers to sending letters or harboring affection in the heart. Allah knows what you conceal and what you declare. Whoever violates this prohibition has strayed from the right path and led himself to ruin.\n\nIn the second verse, it is described what the enemies would do should they overcome you: they would stretch out their hands and their tongues against you with evil, seek to kill and capture you, and moreover wish that you would disbelieve. This is a frightening warning demonstrating how far their hostility could go.\n\nThe third and fourth verses inform that relatives and children will be of no benefit on the Day of Resurrection, and that Allah will judge you according to your deeds. For this reason, believers must not take the enemies of Allah as allies and must strictly refrain from disclosing secrets to them in times of war.\n\nIn the fifth verse, a prayer is taught: 'Our Lord, make us not a trial for the disbelievers.' That is, do not cause us to be defeated by them lest they look down upon faith. In the sixth verse, it is commanded to take Abraham (Ibrahim) and his companions as an example; they had clearly declared enmity to their people. One should not pin one's hopes on anyone other than Allah.\n\nIn the seventh and eighth verses, the glad tidings are given that if one stays completely away from enemies and strives (engages in jihad), Allah may turn hostility into friendship. Indeed, after the conquest of Mecca, many enemies became Muslims. However, the ninth verse strictly forbids establishing friendship with those who fight against you, expel you from your homes, and aid in your expulsion; those who do so are the wrongdoers.\n\nFrom the tenth verse onward, it is commanded that migrating believing women be tested, that they not be sent back to their disbelieving husbands, and that their mahr (dowries) be refunded. It is stated that believing men should not hold disbelieving women in marriage bonds and should ask back the mahr they spent. In the final verses, the terms of allegiance for women (not associating partners with Allah, not stealing, not committing adultery, not killing their children, not bringing slander, and not disobeying the Prophet in what is good) are explained, and Allah's forgiveness is emphasized.\n\nIn the 10th verse of the surah, it is commanded that believing women who have migrated be tested. Allah knows best about their faith. If it is recognized that they are believers, they must not be sent back to the disbelievers. For these women are not lawful for the disbelievers, nor are the disbelievers lawful for them. What their husbands gave as mahr must be returned. Believing men may marry these women once they have given them their mahr. It is not permissible to retain disbelieving women in marital bonds; the mahr spent on them should be demanded, and they too may demand what they spent. This is the judgment of Allah.\n\nIn the 11th verse, if any of the wives of the believers flee to the disbelievers and the believers prevail in battle, it is commanded that those whose wives fled be given from the spoils of war an amount equal to what they had spent as mahr. Believers must be mindful of Allah. These rulings constitute the foundation of Islamic international law and relations.\n\nIn the 12th verse, the conditions for believing women who pledge allegiance (bay'ah) to the Prophet are listed: not associating anything with Allah, not stealing, not committing adultery, not killing their children, not producing a slander fabricated between their hands and feet (that is, not attributing an illegitimate child to the husband), and not disobeying the Prophet in any good deed. The pledge of those who pledge allegiance under these conditions is accepted, and forgiveness is sought for them from Allah.\n\nIn the 13th verse, believers are forbidden from allying with people who have incurred Allah's wrath. Just as the disbelievers have despaired of the occupants of the graves, they have likewise despaired of the Hereafter. Moreover, befriending those who fight on account of religion, expel you from your homes, and assist in your expulsion is also forbidden. This prohibition requires not displaying love and friendship toward them.\n\nIn these verses, there is a subtlety in specifically mentioning 'those who aided in your expulsion': the intention was not to sever ties immediately with those who aid enemies, but rather to allow an opportunity for political initiatives to dissuade them from assisting. Thus, these two verses determine important rules in Islam's international relations. The verses present believers with a test of recognizing friend and foe, and staying away from enemies."